.TH AENIGMA-VPN-SERVER-CLIENT 8 "2026-08-17" "aenigma 5.0.0" "Aenigma Administration Scripts"
.SH NAME
aenigma-vpn-server-client \- issue a new OpenVPN client certificate on the server
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B aenigma-vpn-server-client
.B \-c
.I CLIENT_NAME
.B \-d
.I DOMAIN
.br
.B aenigma-vpn-server-client
.B \-h
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B aenigma-vpn-server-client
issues a new client certificate signed by the shared certificate
authority previously created by
.BR aenigma-vpn-server (8),
and assembles the file bundle a client machine needs to connect: the
client's certificate and private key, the CA certificate, and a copy of
the server's shared TLS-auth key.
.PP
This script is intended to run on the
.B VPN server
machine, where the certificate authority's private key resides. The
resulting bundle is written to
.IB /etc/openvpn/clients/ CLIENT_NAME
and must then be transferred out of band to the client machine, into the
same relative path, before running
.BR aenigma-vpn-client (8)
there.
.PP
.B aenigma-vpn-server-client
must be run as root, since it reads the certificate authority's private
key material and changes ownership of the generated client key files.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-c " CLIENT_NAME"
Identifier for the new client identity, e.g.
.IR john .
Used as the certificate's common name and as the name of the output
directory. Required.
.TP
.BI \-d " DOMAIN"
Name of the previously provisioned VPN server instance (as passed to
.BR aenigma-vpn-server (8)
via its
.B \-d
option) that this client will connect to. Used to locate that server's
shared TLS-auth key. Required.
.TP
.B \-h
Display usage information and exit.
.SH INTERACTIVE PROMPTS
The certificate authority's private key is passphrase-protected. Signing
the new client certificate requires unlocking it, so this script will
interactively prompt for the CA passphrase during
.B sign-req .
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success; the client certificate was issued and its file bundle was
assembled.
.TP
.B 1
Invoked without root privileges, called with insufficient or invalid
arguments, the certificate authority has not been set up yet, the target
server instance's TLS-auth key could not be found, or a certificate
already exists for the given
.IR CLIENT_NAME .
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/openvpn/easy-rsa/pki
Shared Easy-RSA PKI, created by
.BR aenigma-vpn-server (8).
Must already exist; this script does not create a certificate authority.
.TP
.IB /etc/openvpn/ DOMAIN /ta.key
The target server instance's shared TLS-auth key, copied into the client
bundle. Must already exist.
.TP
.IB /etc/openvpn/clients/ CLIENT_NAME
Output directory containing the assembled client bundle:
.BR ca.crt ", "
.IB CLIENT_NAME .crt ", "
.IB CLIENT_NAME .key " (mode 0600, owned by " openvpn "), and "
.BR ta.key " (mode 0600, owned by " openvpn ).
.SH NOTES
.IP \(bu 2
Re-running
.B aenigma-vpn-server-client
for a
.I CLIENT_NAME
that has already been issued a certificate is refused: the script detects
the existing issued certificate and exits with an error rather than
proceeding, since regenerating the certificate request would silently
overwrite the existing private key while the subsequent signing step
would then fail against the already-issued certificate, leaving the PKI
in an inconsistent state. To reissue a certificate for an existing client,
first revoke the existing one using
.BR easyrsa (1)
directly.
.IP \(bu 2
The generated bundle contains unencrypted private key material
(\fICLIENT_NAME\fR.key and \fBta.key\fR). It should be transferred to the
client machine over a secure, out-of-band channel.
.IP \(bu 2
.B umask 077
is set for the duration of the script, so the output directory and files
are created with restricted access from the moment they are written, even
before the explicit
.BR chmod (1)
calls run.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR aenigma-vpn-server (8),
.BR aenigma-vpn-client (8),
.BR easyrsa (1)
.SH AUTHOR
Romulus-Emanuel Ruja <romulus.rujas@aenigma.ro>